1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:05,680
It makes the world go round, and up and down, and side to side.

2
00:00:05,681 --> 00:00:09,120
For us to recover aircraft, we have to have grease.

3
00:00:09,121 --> 00:00:15,560
In our relentless battle to keep moving, grease, in all its forms, is the unsung hero.

4
00:00:15,561 --> 00:00:20,240
Without it, the countless wheels of our lives would grind to a halt.

5
00:00:20,241 --> 00:00:25,840
From aircraft carriers to canals, grease of all kinds keeps everything rolling along,

6
00:00:25,841 --> 00:00:28,680
smooth and fast.

7
00:00:28,681 --> 00:00:30,680
You know what the squeaky wheel gets.

8
00:00:30,681 --> 00:00:32,380
Nice good blob of grease.

9
00:00:32,381 --> 00:00:35,680
It's the untold story of grease on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:53,140
It's a fact of our modern existence.

11
00:00:53,141 --> 00:00:56,140
Not much around us moves without causing heat or friction.

12
00:00:56,141 --> 00:01:01,140
That's why grease is key to the motion of our day-to-day lives.

13
00:01:01,141 --> 00:01:07,140
We find them in many places that we really don't know and see and are aware of,

14
00:01:07,141 --> 00:01:09,140
but they're very essential for everyday life.

15
00:01:09,141 --> 00:01:11,140
While the virtues of grease are countless,

16
00:01:11,141 --> 00:01:14,140
its chief attribute is its semi-solid quality,

17
00:01:14,141 --> 00:01:19,140
which makes it an even more effective lubricant than oil on numerous moving parts.

18
00:01:19,141 --> 00:01:21,340
parts.

19
00:01:21,341 --> 00:01:26,240
Grease is applied to places that are either spinning very fast

20
00:01:26,241 --> 00:01:29,820
or in a place that is very hard to lubricate,

21
00:01:29,821 --> 00:01:33,660
so that a grease will hold the oil there for a long time

22
00:01:33,661 --> 00:01:38,600
or resist being spun off or being washed off with water.

23
00:01:41,660 --> 00:01:43,940
On the USS Nimitz supercarrier,

24
00:01:43,941 --> 00:01:46,540
grease is the lifeline of their most important mission.

25
00:01:46,541 --> 00:01:50,280
There's a million moving parts in the flight deck

26
00:01:50,281 --> 00:01:51,020
in the aircraft carrier.

27
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,720
For us to recover aircraft, we have to have grease.

28
00:01:57,740 --> 00:01:59,740
About a mile off the coast of San Diego,

29
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,660
F-A-18 Hornet fighter jets are in the middle

30
00:02:02,661 --> 00:02:04,600
of flight exercises on board the Nimitz,

31
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,780
taking off and landing every 40 seconds.

32
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:11,920
Traveling at 150 miles an hour,

33
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:13,420
the aircraft hits the deck,

34
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:16,420
catching its tail hook on the carrier's arresting cable.

35
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:20,760
which brings the jet to a screeching halt in 340 feet.

36
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:26,260
Landing on an aircraft carrier is considered the most challenging task in the military.

37
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:36,380
Pilots must hit their mark on a moving runway,

38
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:39,660
sometimes in pitch black night and in all kinds of weather.

39
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:44,920
Every aircraft safe recovery represents an intricate choreography

40
00:02:44,921 --> 00:02:46,400
between man and machine.

41
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:54,860
Aircraft will come in, he's aiming for the center line, he'll engage the cross deck pennant,

42
00:02:54,861 --> 00:02:58,900
pull the cable out from downstairs, the rest of your engine, pull it out, stop, and we'll

43
00:02:58,901 --> 00:03:00,690
retract it back to the next recovery.

44
00:03:00,691 --> 00:03:03,060
A good crew, we can usually get it about every 30

45
00:03:03,061 --> 00:03:04,780
to 45 seconds, every trap.

46
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:16,160
For the Nimitz Air Department crew, that means constant maintenance of the arresting

47
00:03:16,161 --> 00:03:19,760
gear, the deck cables that catch the planes,

48
00:03:19,761 --> 00:03:22,220
and the complex set of mechanics that releases and retracts

49
00:03:22,221 --> 00:03:24,280
them.

50
00:03:24,281 --> 00:03:28,480
The whole system relies on one essential substance, grease,

51
00:03:28,481 --> 00:03:29,920
and lots of it.

52
00:03:29,921 --> 00:03:32,400
When we're flying heavy, we're looking at about 20 gallons

53
00:03:32,401 --> 00:03:33,480
a day.

54
00:03:33,481 --> 00:03:35,400
The greasing starts with a thick wire

55
00:03:35,401 --> 00:03:38,460
the jet's tail hook engages upon landing.

56
00:03:38,461 --> 00:03:41,600
Each jet must catch one of these four cables,

57
00:03:41,601 --> 00:03:44,120
called a cross-deck pennant.

58
00:03:44,121 --> 00:03:46,160
The diameter is 1 7 sixteenths of an inch.

59
00:03:46,340 --> 00:03:48,060
There's a fiber core in the center of this

60
00:03:48,061 --> 00:03:49,720
that we will go ahead and lubricate,

61
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:52,120
make sure that we have enough cushioning and prevent corrosion.

62
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:53,340
We want this wire to last.

63
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:55,980
As the aircraft catches the cross-deck pennant,

64
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:58,060
the force of its forward motion

65
00:03:58,061 --> 00:04:00,300
transfers to the second part of the arresting wire,

66
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:02,040
the purchase cable.

67
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:05,560
This stretch of cable coming out through the flight deck

68
00:04:05,561 --> 00:04:07,560
is the purchase cable itself.

69
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:10,820
This is what controls everything downstairs.

70
00:04:10,821 --> 00:04:12,620
This is what is fed through our arresting gear engine.

71
00:04:12,621 --> 00:04:18,660
We lubricate this cable daily, daily between flight ops, constantly.

72
00:04:18,661 --> 00:04:19,660
We got to lubricate this.

73
00:04:19,661 --> 00:04:21,660
These cables are really precious to us.

74
00:04:21,661 --> 00:04:23,660
They're only good for 2,000 traps right out of the box.

75
00:04:23,661 --> 00:04:26,660
The more life we get out of these, the better off we're going to be.

76
00:04:26,661 --> 00:04:33,660
The purchase cable coils around a high-speed shiv that releases and retracts the cable on deck.

77
00:04:33,661 --> 00:04:41,660
The crew must grease these shivs every 50 arrestments to counteract the intense heat generated from the heavy torque on the cable.

78
00:04:41,661 --> 00:04:48,420
For this, they use a highly specialized blend of grease that can withstand extreme cold

79
00:04:48,421 --> 00:04:53,620
and hot temperatures from negative 50 to 250 degrees Fahrenheit.

80
00:04:53,621 --> 00:04:56,900
It's reddish in color, it's really thick.

81
00:04:56,901 --> 00:05:01,040
No matter how fast the piece of equipment is moving, the lubrication in there is not going

82
00:05:01,041 --> 00:05:10,160
to bind up.

83
00:05:10,161 --> 00:05:15,800
Each cable on deck is directly connected to its own arresting gear engine down below.

84
00:05:15,801 --> 00:05:18,940
This is the Mark VII Mod III arresting gear engine.

85
00:05:18,941 --> 00:05:24,700
This is what actually controls the stopping of the aircraft once it engages the wires.

86
00:05:24,701 --> 00:05:30,140
Down here, the arresting cable from up top weaves around a crosshead mechanism.

87
00:05:30,141 --> 00:05:34,660
So when an aircraft engages the arresting cable, the forward thrust of it triggers the

88
00:05:34,661 --> 00:05:38,800
crosshead down below to slide across a track.

89
00:05:38,801 --> 00:05:42,940
The crosshead allows the release of the cable that brings the jet to a halt.

90
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:49,380
It happens so fast, creates so much heat that we really couldn't operate without the grease.

91
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:50,340
We need to have it.

92
00:05:51,060 --> 00:05:52,240
We grease these tracks constantly.

93
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:54,400
We grease them in the morning before we start fly operations.

94
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:56,120
We grease them at night when we're done.

95
00:05:56,380 --> 00:06:00,320
Typically in a day, we go through about three gallons just on the track.

96
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:04,460
Meanwhile, a pneumatic system pumps grease to the rest of the engine,

97
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:07,580
keeping the bearing slick and reducing the heat.

98
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:15,660
The lubrication of each of the moving parts is so crucial that even the greasing system itself has an emergency override.

99
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:19,160
Say we lose air pressure in the ship system.

100
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:22,860
We can go in here on these blocks. They have Zerk fittings.

101
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:30,140
We can go in and manually pump this grease in, ensuring that we continue to have grease going into these ships so they don't wear out.

102
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:38,580
Grease consumption on board a supercarrier like the USS Nimitz is so high, the thousands of gallons are always in stock.

103
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:40,800
Grease is on our never-out list.

104
00:06:40,801 --> 00:06:44,800
This is where we store approximately 22,000 gallons of grease.

105
00:06:44,801 --> 00:06:47,800
Seen here is one of the more popular greases.

106
00:06:47,801 --> 00:06:49,800
This is for the arresting gear.

107
00:06:49,801 --> 00:06:51,800
This is a high-demand grease.

108
00:06:51,801 --> 00:06:55,800
But before this grease is loaded up to coat all moving parts of the Nimitz,

109
00:06:55,801 --> 00:07:01,800
it's manufactured to exact specifications at facilities on land.

110
00:07:01,801 --> 00:07:05,800
At grease factories, base oils are cooked and mixed with additives

111
00:07:05,801 --> 00:07:10,040
with additives to make greases for countless applications.

112
00:07:10,041 --> 00:07:11,300
It's a lot of science.

113
00:07:11,301 --> 00:07:14,500
It's a lot of, some people call it art.

114
00:07:14,501 --> 00:07:17,780
Lubricating Specialties Company in Southern California

115
00:07:17,781 --> 00:07:21,240
churns out some 40 million pounds of grease every year

116
00:07:21,241 --> 00:07:24,760
for about 400 different kinds of applications.

117
00:07:24,761 --> 00:07:28,860
Our number one grease is actually a lithium complex grease.

118
00:07:28,861 --> 00:07:32,000
And it's really kind of a multi-purpose grease.

119
00:07:32,001 --> 00:07:34,000
It's used on construction equipment,

120
00:07:34,001 --> 00:07:36,300
heavy-duty trucks, things of that nature.

121
00:07:36,301 --> 00:07:41,300
And you probably didn't know that what makes your power windows work requires a grease

122
00:07:41,301 --> 00:07:44,300
that's also made here.

123
00:07:44,301 --> 00:07:49,500
But no matter the type of grease, each batch here starts with a petroleum oil base that's

124
00:07:49,501 --> 00:07:53,760
heated and cooked at over 300 degrees Fahrenheit.

125
00:07:53,761 --> 00:07:59,400
Cooking the oil causes it to saponify or turn to soap, the foundation of all greases.

126
00:07:59,401 --> 00:08:02,840
And it's not much different than the soap you use at home,

127
00:08:02,841 --> 00:08:07,640
except we use petroleum oils instead of nice oils for our skin.

128
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:11,380
The batch they're cooking here will be a lithium complex,

129
00:08:11,640 --> 00:08:13,880
which makes for the most versatile kinds of greases.

130
00:08:15,940 --> 00:08:20,480
The freshly cooked soap is then pumped in through hoses into giant kettles.

131
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:26,120
They now mix in the additives to give the grease specific attributes.

132
00:08:26,121 --> 00:08:32,260
We have about 30 different additives that we can choose from.

133
00:08:32,261 --> 00:08:38,740
We can add anti-wear additives, we can enhance its water resistance for things that go underwater

134
00:08:38,741 --> 00:08:44,660
or flooded with a lot of water like in steel mills, things of that nature.

135
00:08:44,661 --> 00:08:50,460
As a final touch, they add color, different hues for different types of grease.

136
00:08:50,461 --> 00:09:04,461
The finished product is then pumped down below for packaging in containers small and large.

137
00:09:05,020 --> 00:09:09,980
Greasemaking today must constantly adapt as new technologies demand different and more

138
00:09:09,981 --> 00:09:15,200
specialized lubrication.

139
00:09:15,201 --> 00:09:18,280
for about 4,000 years, the lube business

140
00:09:18,281 --> 00:09:21,100
relied pretty much on two natural elements,

141
00:09:21,101 --> 00:09:24,600
animal fat or vegetable oil.

142
00:09:24,601 --> 00:09:26,860
Chariots found in ancient Egyptian tombs

143
00:09:26,861 --> 00:09:29,960
hold remnants of animal-based lubricants,

144
00:09:29,961 --> 00:09:32,280
probably from beef or mutton.

145
00:09:32,281 --> 00:09:35,640
And ancient Roman texts tell of greasing their wagon axles

146
00:09:35,641 --> 00:09:38,140
with lubricants made from olives or pig fat.

147
00:09:38,141 --> 00:09:47,380
New mechanisms emerged in the medieval ages, followed centuries later by the explosion of

148
00:09:47,381 --> 00:09:49,533
the Industrial Revolution.

149
00:09:49,534 --> 00:09:54,500
But even well into the 19th century, moving parts were still

150
00:09:54,501 --> 00:10:01,340
being greased with animal or vegetable-based compounds, until a groundbreaking discovery

151
00:10:01,341 --> 00:10:04,140
in the late 1850s, petroleum oil.

152
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:08,720
Scientists quickly learned how to manipulate

153
00:10:08,721 --> 00:10:10,880
the mineral oil base with different chemicals

154
00:10:10,881 --> 00:10:12,520
to make specialized lubricants.

155
00:10:14,580 --> 00:10:17,480
Since then, petroleum products have greased it all.

156
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:22,300
Our cars, our planes, and our ships.

157
00:10:24,060 --> 00:10:26,660
On the USS Nimitz, a 40-pound container

158
00:10:26,661 --> 00:10:29,300
of a resting gear grease has been called up to duty.

159
00:10:29,301 --> 00:10:34,300
Unfortunately for our sailors, there is no high-tech method to get this up.

160
00:10:34,301 --> 00:10:39,300
It goes over the shoulder, it goes up eight decks.

161
00:10:39,301 --> 00:10:49,300
Greece is always in high demand on deck, where the launch crew performs regular equipment maintenance between flight exercises.

162
00:10:49,301 --> 00:10:59,374
These jet blast deflectors rise up behind each plane before a launch to protect equipment and personnel from the jet exhaust that shoots out at 2,300 degrees

163
00:10:59,375 --> 00:11:00,080
Fahrenheit.

164
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:15,020
Frequent greasing of these key launch elements ensures they'll work smoothly and quickly during flight exercises when every second counts.

165
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:30,400
In a world of moving parts of all kinds, most would say grease is the key to success.

166
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:35,640
If we keep it well lubricated, it'll hold up.

167
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:37,980
So if we take care of it, it'll take care of us.

168
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:44,500
On the flight deck of the USS Nimitz supercarrier, the topside crew is greasing the launch catapults,

169
00:11:44,501 --> 00:11:49,640
the piston-driven mechanism that propels the jets forward at blistering speeds.

170
00:11:50,820 --> 00:11:53,340
We'll grease this every 25 shots.

171
00:11:54,380 --> 00:11:58,640
The steam-powered catapults allow aircraft to take off from a carrier's short runway

172
00:11:58,641 --> 00:12:01,860
by literally shooting the planes off the deck.

173
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:05,720
This right here is the nose gear launch in GL.

174
00:12:06,580 --> 00:12:10,180
What happens is the aircraft will stop in this general area,

175
00:12:10,181 --> 00:12:14,380
they'll drop their launch bar, and it'll taxi on through.

176
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:17,060
and actuate these hooks right here.

177
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:20,540
Just below deck lie two giant steam cylinders,

178
00:12:21,180 --> 00:12:24,800
each the length of a football field that encase two pistons.

179
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:29,140
Once a plane hooks in, these cylinders fill with steam,

180
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:32,560
the pressure adjusted to the type of plane and weather conditions.

181
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:36,620
When it's all systems go, the pistons are released,

182
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:38,740
brusting the shuttle and plane forward

183
00:12:38,741 --> 00:12:43,200
from zero to 160 miles an hour in two seconds.

184
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:47,500
Power, combat power, thumbs up.

185
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:56,500
The shuttle transfers the energy from the steam pistons to the aircraft.

186
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:03,500
I've been shot off and, oh, it takes the wind out of you.

187
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:11,500
Up to six times a day, the crew lubricates each catapult shuttle

188
00:13:11,501 --> 00:13:13,500
with general purpose grease.

189
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:17,000
10 shots of it at a time.

190
00:13:17,001 --> 00:13:19,300
But below deck, where the high-pressure steam

191
00:13:19,301 --> 00:13:22,100
cylinders are, greasing requires a much more

192
00:13:22,101 --> 00:13:23,700
specialized substance.

193
00:13:23,701 --> 00:13:26,800
It's a high-temperature catapult grease made by Bell Ray.

194
00:13:26,801 --> 00:13:30,640
And without this grease, we cannot shoot these catapults.

195
00:13:30,641 --> 00:13:32,960
The catapult grease will hold up in temperatures

196
00:13:32,961 --> 00:13:36,400
up to 500 degrees, a key requirement

197
00:13:36,401 --> 00:13:39,200
in this hot steam environment.

198
00:13:39,201 --> 00:13:41,800
But its tenacity comes with a price.

199
00:13:41,801 --> 00:13:46,700
It has a unique smell that you never forget if you get it on you.

200
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:50,120
But that doesn't stop the daily greasing that happens down here

201
00:13:50,121 --> 00:13:53,260
to prevent corrosion that could impede the launch valves.

202
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:00,220
For the launch valves alone,

203
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:03,620
the Nimitz goes through about five gallons of grease a week.

204
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:08,700
It's all part of what keeps this gigantic floating city

205
00:14:08,701 --> 00:14:11,000
greased up from stem to stern.

206
00:14:11,800 --> 00:14:18,800
The massive scale of the Nimitz reflects its extraordinary capabilities on the high seas.

207
00:14:18,801 --> 00:14:26,800
But because of its size, the Nimitz won't ever fit through this ocean passageway.

208
00:14:26,801 --> 00:14:34,800
Every day, some 35 vessels, 106 feet wide or less, squeeze into the locks of the Panama Canal.

209
00:14:34,801 --> 00:14:40,140
Here they rise a total of about 85 feet into this remarkable shortcut between the Atlantic

210
00:14:40,141 --> 00:14:49,720
and Pacific Oceans, and lower back down to sea level on the other side, 52 miles later.

211
00:14:49,721 --> 00:14:54,280
When a ship enters the first chamber of the locks, the gates close behind it.

212
00:14:54,281 --> 00:14:58,420
Water fills the chamber, raising the ship up to the level of the next chamber.

213
00:14:58,421 --> 00:15:02,200
The gates open and the ship moves on.

214
00:15:02,201 --> 00:15:07,200
The locks' colossal gates are the canal's most impressive set

215
00:15:07,201 --> 00:15:09,200
of moving parts.

216
00:15:09,201 --> 00:15:13,200
We have uses for grease everywhere.

217
00:15:13,201 --> 00:15:18,200
The hinges on the gates, the connection of the arm on the

218
00:15:18,201 --> 00:15:23,200
gate, these connections pivot, and they need more grease.

219
00:15:23,201 --> 00:15:26,200
Today, the gates are controlled by a hydraulic system located

220
00:15:26,201 --> 00:15:28,200
underneath the locks.

221
00:15:32,200 --> 00:15:36,780
This hydraulic retractable arm opens and closes the gates of the locks.

222
00:15:38,360 --> 00:15:41,380
When the arm extends, it pushes the gate open.

223
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:45,000
When it retracts, it pulls it shut.

224
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:51,560
Because hydraulic oil flows through this system, it conveniently lubricates itself.

225
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:56,720
We don't expect to change the oil of the hydraulic system in 25 years

226
00:15:56,721 --> 00:16:00,480
because we keep regular maintenance on the health of the oil.

227
00:16:00,481 --> 00:16:06,180
The ease of maintaining a hydraulic system is a fairly new asset to the lock's operation.

228
00:16:07,580 --> 00:16:10,760
Until 2002, when the hydraulics fully took over,

229
00:16:11,320 --> 00:16:15,140
the gates opened and shut using the original gear system from 1914,

230
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:17,100
when the canal first opened.

231
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:22,780
Back then, a 40-horsepower electric motor powered a set of reduction gears

232
00:16:22,781 --> 00:16:27,720
that set in motion a massive bull gear, also known as a Panama wheel.

233
00:16:27,721 --> 00:16:34,720
The rotation of the Panama Wheel moved the arm that opened and shut each gate.

234
00:16:34,721 --> 00:16:39,720
More metal moving against metal called for a much bigger grease job.

235
00:16:39,721 --> 00:16:48,720
When we had the bull here in this room, we had a team of oilers that would come in and grease the whole mechanical system.

236
00:16:48,721 --> 00:16:53,720
They had to do this about twice a week as a minimum, sometimes more.

237
00:16:53,721 --> 00:16:57,720
These gears were the largest gears, 23 foot in diameter,

238
00:16:57,721 --> 00:17:01,720
that would move the gates of the Panama Canal.

239
00:17:01,721 --> 00:17:04,720
This is the original design, 1914.

240
00:17:04,721 --> 00:17:08,720
Remnants of the old system are a rare find these days.

241
00:17:08,721 --> 00:17:11,720
Look at the size of these guys.

242
00:17:11,721 --> 00:17:14,720
This is residue of the grease that we used to use,

243
00:17:14,721 --> 00:17:15,720
hardened and worn out.

244
00:17:15,721 --> 00:17:18,720
This lubrication would protect the teeth on the gear

245
00:17:18,721 --> 00:17:21,720
and the wear on the gate altogether.

246
00:17:21,721 --> 00:17:23,720
It's been a while, old friend.

247
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:33,720
As well greased as it was, the ingeniously designed gear system turned efficiently for almost 90 years,

248
00:17:33,721 --> 00:17:36,720
allowing passageway to some 800,000 vessels.

249
00:17:36,721 --> 00:17:43,720
Cargo ships, cruise ships, pleasure boats, and warships alike all crossed through the Panama Canal,

250
00:17:43,721 --> 00:17:48,720
saving themselves the 8,000-mile trek around the tip of South America.

251
00:17:48,721 --> 00:17:57,400
Today, thanks to the new hydraulic technology, the Panama Canal locks require about 50% less

252
00:17:57,401 --> 00:18:01,660
grease than they did in their gear-driven days.

253
00:18:01,661 --> 00:18:15,661
But in a different part of the canal, one machine is using more grease than ever before.

254
00:18:17,280 --> 00:18:25,220
The Rialto Christensen is one of the biggest dipper dredges in the world, and has been scooping up earth from the Panama Canal for over 30 years.

255
00:18:27,580 --> 00:18:37,200
In one day, this dredge's giant cable-driven bucket will dig up to 9,000 cubic yards of month, enough to fill about 600 dump trucks.

256
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:46,960
That's key to the Panama Canal widening program, an ambitious plan that could double ship traffic by 2014.

257
00:18:47,280 --> 00:18:56,920
With that kind of deadline, the Rialto Christensen works 24-7, scooping up debris in three back-to-back

258
00:18:56,921 --> 00:18:57,621
ships.

259
00:18:59,060 --> 00:19:01,800
It's working very, very, very, very good.

260
00:19:02,580 --> 00:19:04,320
The key here is the maintenance.

261
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:07,320
And that's one greasy job.

262
00:19:11,460 --> 00:19:16,320
At the end of every ship, crews descend upon the dredge to grease it from top to bottom.

263
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:20,840
Without the grease, we will be in trouble.

264
00:19:21,840 --> 00:19:28,140
Three times a day, the 65-foot boom that moves the bucket up and down gets a full grease down.

265
00:19:31,240 --> 00:19:37,480
For the dredge's exposed parts, the crew uses a thick, high-temperature grease that's also waterproof,

266
00:19:38,020 --> 00:19:42,060
so it can withstand both the tropical heat and torrential rains of Panama.

267
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:46,980
For each ship, they go through about eight pounds of the stuff.

268
00:19:47,280 --> 00:19:49,120
to completely coat the dredge's arm.

269
00:19:53,880 --> 00:19:57,520
To keep the entire topside platform rotating smoothly,

270
00:19:57,521 --> 00:20:00,480
the crew regularly lubricates the massive horizontal wheel

271
00:20:00,481 --> 00:20:02,760
it sits on, called the slewing circle.

272
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:08,800
Here, they use a pneumatic system

273
00:20:08,801 --> 00:20:10,240
to hit every lube point directly.

274
00:20:13,520 --> 00:20:16,520
Inside the slewing circle, each of the wheel's steel

275
00:20:16,521 --> 00:20:20,597
head pins must be greased. 99 of them, one at a time.

276
00:20:20,598 --> 00:20:23,520
These fixed pins transmit the rotating

277
00:20:24,180 --> 00:20:26,502
movement to the platform.

278
00:20:26,503 --> 00:20:32,260
Inside the engine room, the motor that runs the slewing circle

279
00:20:32,261 --> 00:20:34,593
gets its daily move job too.

280
00:20:34,594 --> 00:20:39,260
As do the huge cables that hoist the bucket up and down,

281
00:20:39,261 --> 00:20:41,680
and the gears that make them work.

282
00:20:43,840 --> 00:20:46,140
At the end of its 24-hour day,

283
00:20:46,740 --> 00:20:48,680
the Rialto Christensen and all its parts

284
00:20:48,681 --> 00:20:51,500
require about 12 pounds of grease to do the job.

285
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:55,040
That's almost 4,400 pounds every year.

286
00:20:57,020 --> 00:21:01,360
It's no wonder this is a popular item on board.

287
00:21:01,361 --> 00:21:11,600
If Greece makes the world go round,

288
00:21:11,601 --> 00:21:14,300
it's fitting that a place that connects two oceans

289
00:21:14,301 --> 00:21:16,800
would need a lot of it.

290
00:21:16,801 --> 00:21:22,240
Something as simple as Greece, it makes this lot go.

291
00:21:22,241 --> 00:21:24,560
The mules of the Panama Canal,

292
00:21:24,561 --> 00:21:26,980
electric-powered locomotives that guide the ships

293
00:21:26,981 --> 00:21:31,360
in and out of the locks, need a regular grease down.

294
00:21:31,361 --> 00:21:38,360
Giant cables connect the mules to every vessel as it enters the canal.

295
00:21:38,361 --> 00:21:43,000
While the ships move forward through the locks on their own power, the mules control their

296
00:21:43,001 --> 00:21:47,160
side-to-side motion in the extremely narrow chambers.

297
00:21:47,161 --> 00:21:52,380
The mules have been part of the canal since it opened in 1914, guiding ships in and out

298
00:21:52,381 --> 00:21:59,440
of the locks 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.

299
00:21:59,441 --> 00:22:04,360
To release and retract the cables that connect to the ships, the locomotives house a hydraulically

300
00:22:04,361 --> 00:22:08,120
powered set of gears, called a windless unit.

301
00:22:08,121 --> 00:22:14,440
This piece of machinery moves with the cable as it's being paid out.

302
00:22:14,441 --> 00:22:18,280
All the gears need to be greased.

303
00:22:18,281 --> 00:22:23,820
A regularly lubricated internal gear locks the mule down to the tracks, which get greased

304
00:22:23,821 --> 00:22:25,200
every eight weeks.

305
00:22:25,201 --> 00:22:32,200
It's pulling 70,000 tons of force, so it needs the rack for traction.

306
00:22:32,201 --> 00:22:40,200
It's not unlike any other track system, even one that's just for fun or beer.

307
00:22:40,201 --> 00:22:54,201
At Six Flags Discovery Kingdom in Vallejo, California, Greece is what keeps the thrill

308
00:22:55,540 --> 00:23:00,700
seekers thrilled.

309
00:23:00,701 --> 00:23:08,200
The Vertical Velocity, or V2, shoots a seven-car train on a steel track up a 150-foot spike,

310
00:23:08,201 --> 00:23:09,860
forwards and backwards.

311
00:23:09,861 --> 00:23:11,640
You know, there's a lot of facets to it

312
00:23:11,641 --> 00:23:13,200
that make this thing work.

313
00:23:13,201 --> 00:23:16,780
You've got the assembly up here with the wheel carriers on it,

314
00:23:16,781 --> 00:23:18,600
and you've got different types of wheels,

315
00:23:18,601 --> 00:23:20,560
all of which require grease.

316
00:23:20,561 --> 00:23:22,040
Each wheel has its own zert fitting,

317
00:23:22,041 --> 00:23:24,260
and basically they hook a grease gun up to it

318
00:23:24,261 --> 00:23:27,240
and pump the grease into it, and it's a paste.

319
00:23:27,241 --> 00:23:31,060
And basically it decreases friction and heat.

320
00:23:31,061 --> 00:23:34,820
This mainly keeps these wheels sliding

321
00:23:34,821 --> 00:23:36,580
along the surface of the track.

322
00:23:36,581 --> 00:23:41,140
If they bite to the track too hard, they grip the track too hard, they'll get hot.

323
00:23:41,800 --> 00:23:46,100
And the rubber on the wheel will literally come off after a while of doing that.

324
00:23:49,060 --> 00:23:52,940
The V2 accelerates to 70 miles per hour in four seconds,

325
00:23:53,340 --> 00:23:58,700
using cutting-edge technology to get an extra boost of speed every time it passes through the station.

326
00:24:01,840 --> 00:24:05,520
But for more traditional adrenaline junkies, there's the roar,

327
00:24:05,521 --> 00:24:08,340
a wooden coaster on a steel-plated track

328
00:24:08,341 --> 00:24:10,820
that gains speed the old-fashioned way.

329
00:24:11,740 --> 00:24:12,860
It's a gravity ride.

330
00:24:13,040 --> 00:24:15,020
It goes up a chain lift,

331
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:16,960
and then when it drops off, it's gravity all the way around.

332
00:24:22,040 --> 00:24:25,020
We're mixing the graphite lubricant that we use on the track here.

333
00:24:25,021 --> 00:24:27,920
We place it on the steel running rails of the track

334
00:24:27,921 --> 00:24:29,520
to lubricate the ride.

335
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:33,140
The graphite, say, it's a type of lubricant that sticks real well,

336
00:24:33,240 --> 00:24:35,480
won't fling off the ride and get all over everything.

337
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:39,280
You know, it's a good base to allow the wheels to carry it around and stick.

338
00:24:39,760 --> 00:24:40,900
We do a daily inspection.

339
00:24:41,100 --> 00:24:44,520
We walk the track, every square foot of it, check the track out.

340
00:24:44,920 --> 00:24:48,420
And while they're doing that, they're out there looking at the, you know, the lubrication that's on there,

341
00:24:48,460 --> 00:24:51,480
and they apply graphite to the areas that need to be.

342
00:24:51,980 --> 00:24:57,160
The difference in lubricating this train versus the steel train is there's a lot more points to look at.

343
00:24:57,240 --> 00:25:02,300
There's a lot more systems in this ride that have to be looked at from a lubrication standpoint.

344
00:25:02,301 --> 00:25:06,920
And that would be everything from the train itself, the wheels, the axles, those types of things.

345
00:25:07,560 --> 00:25:14,620
Which all makes for a smooth ride up 95 feet and down.

346
00:25:18,500 --> 00:25:22,160
For trains that run a little slower and closer to the ground,

347
00:25:22,880 --> 00:25:26,320
automatic lubricators grease the tracks to minimize heat and friction.

348
00:25:27,060 --> 00:25:30,680
The Iowa Northern Railway Company uses a magnetic wheel counter

349
00:25:30,681 --> 00:25:33,020
to trigger their rail grease dispenser.

350
00:25:34,020 --> 00:25:36,500
We've got the lubricator set so every 12 wheels,

351
00:25:36,660 --> 00:25:38,500
it'll operate for 0.2 seconds,

352
00:25:39,080 --> 00:25:42,120
pumping grease to the wiper rails clamped inside the rails.

353
00:25:42,340 --> 00:25:43,660
And as the grease is pumped,

354
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,780
you'll get a finger of grease coming up through each of the 48 ports,

355
00:25:47,300 --> 00:25:48,980
and the grease will come up on the inside of the rail,

356
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:51,540
and as the wheel passes over, the wheel picks up the grease,

357
00:25:52,040 --> 00:25:54,600
carries it down the rail with it for 5 miles in either direction.

358
00:25:54,601 --> 00:26:00,480
Ten feet from the tracks, the lubricating reservoir stores the railway grease.

359
00:26:00,481 --> 00:26:06,100
Inside this box, we've got our control panel, which controls how much grease we actually

360
00:26:06,101 --> 00:26:07,100
put out.

361
00:26:07,101 --> 00:26:10,380
We adjust both time and the number of wheels that go by to control, and the pump moves

362
00:26:10,381 --> 00:26:15,400
the grease from the reservoir through the hose, up into the railroad tracks, and you

363
00:26:15,401 --> 00:26:18,800
want to reach a perfect balance where you can have just the right amount of grease so

364
00:26:18,801 --> 00:26:23,200
that you don't get over on the top of the rail and create traction issues, but yet put

365
00:26:23,201 --> 00:26:24,840
just enough to spread down the track.

366
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,260
Here comes the train, and what you're going to see is,

367
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:31,120
if you look close, you can see grease actually coming up

368
00:26:31,121 --> 00:26:32,640
through on the inside of the rail.

369
00:26:32,960 --> 00:26:34,440
You can see the grease stretching.

370
00:26:35,420 --> 00:26:39,160
Iowa Northern uses an extreme pressure lithium complex grease

371
00:26:39,161 --> 00:26:41,420
that can handle the heavy load of a freight train.

372
00:26:42,860 --> 00:26:45,020
It's typical of most railway greases,

373
00:26:45,500 --> 00:26:47,340
except for one important difference.

374
00:26:48,300 --> 00:26:50,580
This grease isn't made from petroleum.

375
00:26:51,180 --> 00:26:52,580
It's made from soybean oil.

376
00:26:53,200 --> 00:26:55,300
this soy-based grease biodegrades.

377
00:26:55,301 --> 00:26:59,300
In 28 days, 60% of this grease will be already biodegraded.

378
00:26:59,301 --> 00:27:00,800
So it's better for the railroad tracks

379
00:27:00,801 --> 00:27:03,140
and it's better for the environment.

380
00:27:03,141 --> 00:27:06,600
This bio-based rail grease is the most popular product

381
00:27:06,601 --> 00:27:09,340
of environmental lubricants manufacturing

382
00:27:09,341 --> 00:27:11,100
in nearby Grundy Center, Iowa.

383
00:27:13,640 --> 00:27:17,240
Here they make about 2 million pounds of grease every year

384
00:27:17,241 --> 00:27:20,140
for all kinds of industrial applications,

385
00:27:20,141 --> 00:27:21,660
all from vegetable oils.

386
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:26,780
These products perform equal or better than their petroleum counterparts.

387
00:27:27,600 --> 00:27:30,260
And because the price of petroleum has been going up,

388
00:27:30,620 --> 00:27:33,240
now the price of these products are actually comparable.

389
00:27:33,940 --> 00:27:36,500
Today, every major store carries these products.

390
00:27:38,800 --> 00:27:42,240
ELM greases also make use of a prolific local product.

391
00:27:43,220 --> 00:27:44,680
We're in the middle of Iowa.

392
00:27:44,681 --> 00:27:49,420
We have a tremendous amount of surplus of soybean oil and other vegetable oils.

393
00:27:49,421 --> 00:27:54,680
So our initial mission was to find new uses for our excess soybean oil.

394
00:27:56,080 --> 00:27:59,460
This we call charging the tank, charging the vessel.

395
00:28:00,300 --> 00:28:04,540
Manufacturing grease from vegetable oil is no different than making it from a petroleum base.

396
00:28:05,760 --> 00:28:08,620
We're heating the product and creating soap.

397
00:28:09,400 --> 00:28:12,180
Later down the process will then turn into grease.

398
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:15,460
While the basic greasemaking steps are the same,

399
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:19,380
what's cooking the oil at ELM is a brand new technology.

400
00:28:19,420 --> 00:28:24,140
that uses a heat source found in most American kitchens, microwaves.

401
00:28:24,720 --> 00:28:27,420
It's really very similar to your home unit.

402
00:28:27,640 --> 00:28:31,980
It's just a magnitude of larger, much, much more power.

403
00:28:32,740 --> 00:28:35,660
Just like a kitchen microwave, the crew punches a touchscreen

404
00:28:35,661 --> 00:28:38,920
to select the specific temperature and power output they need

405
00:28:38,921 --> 00:28:40,980
to cook up their batch of grease.

406
00:28:41,160 --> 00:28:45,860
There's a power supply in here that puts out about 17,500 volts

407
00:28:45,861 --> 00:28:49,800
that's feeding into a magnetron, just like your home unit.

408
00:28:50,180 --> 00:28:53,300
That energy is then transported through that waveguide

409
00:28:53,301 --> 00:28:55,160
over to the applicator

410
00:28:55,161 --> 00:28:58,280
and then dispersed into the product inside that tank.

411
00:28:58,840 --> 00:29:01,660
The microwave application is so much more efficient.

412
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:02,740
It's much safer.

413
00:29:02,900 --> 00:29:05,080
It's a lot easier to teach people to use,

414
00:29:05,220 --> 00:29:07,580
actually taking about a third as much time

415
00:29:07,581 --> 00:29:09,480
as the conventional method of heating.

416
00:29:10,320 --> 00:29:12,380
Which allows ELM to produce

417
00:29:12,381 --> 00:29:15,780
more high-performance bio-based greases faster.

418
00:29:15,860 --> 00:29:16,564
and cheaper.

419
00:29:16,565 --> 00:29:21,140
These products are going to not only bring us back to a healthier environment,

420
00:29:21,460 --> 00:29:23,880
but reduce our dependence on imported oil.

421
00:29:23,881 --> 00:29:26,300
So with bio-based lubes making a comeback,

422
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:29,580
watch for more vegetable oils greasing our wheels.

423
00:29:34,500 --> 00:29:38,040
We've seen how greases of all kinds help the world go round.

424
00:29:40,300 --> 00:29:43,940
But what about those that make it go up and down?

425
00:29:45,860 --> 00:29:51,500
On the USS Nimitz supercarrier, huge cable-operative platforms raise jets 100 feet from the bay

426
00:29:51,501 --> 00:29:58,360
hangar to the flight deck in less than 15 seconds.

427
00:29:58,361 --> 00:30:04,520
The Nimitz has four of these giant elevators, each with a 130,000-pound capacity that can

428
00:30:04,521 --> 00:30:07,460
hold two fighter jets at a time.

429
00:30:07,461 --> 00:30:12,380
The nine sets of heavy cables that pull the elevators require regular lubricating with

430
00:30:12,381 --> 00:30:16,580
with specialized wire rope grease.

431
00:30:16,581 --> 00:30:22,280
The greasing needs are similar to any elevator system that runs on cables.

432
00:30:22,281 --> 00:30:27,140
In this auto dealership in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, is a cargo elevator that's been running for

433
00:30:27,141 --> 00:30:31,220
more than 100 years.

434
00:30:31,221 --> 00:30:32,220
It's early 1900s.

435
00:30:32,221 --> 00:30:35,880
There's not too many of these around anymore.

436
00:30:35,881 --> 00:30:41,000
Age in all, this elevator works like new, thanks to one substance.

437
00:30:41,001 --> 00:30:43,680
As you can see, there's grease everywhere.

438
00:30:43,681 --> 00:30:45,680
You need grease all over this unit.

439
00:30:45,681 --> 00:30:47,800
There's moving parts.

440
00:30:47,801 --> 00:30:54,860
It's really old, and it's the old-fashioned, hands-on type of grease applications.

441
00:30:54,861 --> 00:31:00,980
This part of the elevator here is called the drum, and you have four sets of cables.

442
00:31:00,981 --> 00:31:07,120
Two go down to the counterweights, and two come over to the car.

443
00:31:07,121 --> 00:31:11,520
As the drum turns, one set of cables winds up

444
00:31:11,521 --> 00:31:13,300
while the other set unwinds.

445
00:31:13,301 --> 00:31:15,600
That's what makes the elevator go up and down.

446
00:31:15,601 --> 00:31:19,860
Without greasing this properly and maintaining it properly,

447
00:31:19,861 --> 00:31:21,660
it would never last this long.

448
00:31:21,661 --> 00:31:23,240
Just give it a couple shots,

449
00:31:24,940 --> 00:31:26,480
till it starts oozing out.

450
00:31:26,481 --> 00:31:28,340
Now you know you have enough grease in there.

451
00:31:28,341 --> 00:31:31,380
You're pushing the old out and getting fresh grease in.

452
00:31:31,381 --> 00:31:37,380
Marshall Elevator rebuilt basically this whole system.

453
00:31:37,381 --> 00:31:41,380
We put some new doors on and rebuilt everything in this room,

454
00:31:41,381 --> 00:31:45,380
and they wanted to maintain the older equipment.

455
00:31:45,381 --> 00:31:50,380
It's held up through good maintenance and keeping it well greased.

456
00:31:50,381 --> 00:31:54,380
This historic elevator is part of countless systems old and new,

457
00:31:54,381 --> 00:31:58,380
maintained by Marshall Elevator in downtown Pittsburgh.

458
00:31:58,381 --> 00:32:03,380
This major distributor installs and services elevators for a wide range of clients.

459
00:32:04,380 --> 00:32:09,380
But Marshall began as a manufacturer of elevators almost 200 years ago.

460
00:32:10,380 --> 00:32:12,380
Marshall Elevator was founded in 1818.

461
00:32:13,380 --> 00:32:20,380
Back in the 1840s, we had primitive hoisting mechanisms, chain-driven hoisting machines,

462
00:32:21,380 --> 00:32:25,380
which later evolved into steam-powered vertical systems.

463
00:32:25,381 --> 00:32:31,380
While Marshall no longer makes elevators today, they service and maintain the ones they install.

464
00:32:31,381 --> 00:32:36,380
This is the maintenance machine shop and we do a lot of maintenance on the current elevators.

465
00:32:36,381 --> 00:32:41,380
Marshall's maintenance team still works out of the company's old machine room to fix elevator parts,

466
00:32:41,381 --> 00:32:44,380
using a line shaft that's over a hundred years old.

467
00:32:48,380 --> 00:32:54,380
The line shaft here has one electric motor that drives several machinery off of one motor itself.

468
00:32:55,380 --> 00:33:03,920
And we have a lot of grease and oil that has to be put down to the stuff to make it run smoothly all the time.

469
00:33:04,160 --> 00:33:05,320
That's why it's 100 years old.

470
00:33:08,500 --> 00:33:14,540
The metal cutting lathe that trims elevator parts gets lubed every couple of months with a high temperature grease

471
00:33:14,541 --> 00:33:17,160
that won't melt from the heat of the rotating gears.

472
00:33:18,480 --> 00:33:24,020
There's no grease gun to go deep up into that, so we do it by hand to make sure all the gears are well lubricated.

473
00:33:24,021 --> 00:33:31,020
Farther down the line shaft, a machine called a planer levels out the bed plate that's the base of an elevator.

474
00:33:31,021 --> 00:33:37,020
This gear-operative mechanism needs greasing only every few decades.

475
00:33:37,021 --> 00:33:44,020
You're in for a treat today because after 35 years, I have to re-grease the bull gear.

476
00:33:44,021 --> 00:33:50,020
This old grease is black and it's very stiff and very hard to work with.

477
00:33:50,021 --> 00:33:55,100
The new grease here is a lot more pliable, but it has a lot of viscosity to it.

478
00:33:55,520 --> 00:33:58,780
There's a nice, good blob of grease, okay?

479
00:33:59,140 --> 00:34:03,980
And we want to smear it nice, evenly over the bull gear.

480
00:34:04,420 --> 00:34:10,160
If I didn't clean my hand over the next 35 years, this grease would be the same grease as it's now 35 years from now.

481
00:34:10,180 --> 00:34:11,500
I could retire with the grease on my hand.

482
00:34:12,620 --> 00:34:17,020
For all the elevators that we currently maintain, grease will always need to be used.

483
00:34:17,021 --> 00:34:24,020
Every elevator system and vertical transportation system in the world requires grease and always will.

484
00:34:24,021 --> 00:34:30,020
This PNC Bank building in downtown Pittsburgh is a modern 23-story high-rise

485
00:34:30,021 --> 00:34:33,020
with an elevator system that Marshall's crew services regularly.

486
00:34:33,021 --> 00:34:37,020
This is the most state-of-the-art elevator equipment out there right now.

487
00:34:37,021 --> 00:34:41,020
The machine room is on the top floor above the elevator shaft.

488
00:34:41,021 --> 00:34:44,020
This is a geared traction elevator.

489
00:34:44,021 --> 00:34:51,900
Here, traction steel cables loop around a shiv holding the elevator car on one side and

490
00:34:51,901 --> 00:34:53,980
counterweights on the other.

491
00:34:53,981 --> 00:34:59,500
An electric motor powers the shiv, lowering the elevator when it turns one way and raising

492
00:34:59,501 --> 00:35:04,020
it when it turns the other way.

493
00:35:04,021 --> 00:35:08,740
The traction cables are pre-lubricated with hemp seed oil to prevent wear from running

494
00:35:08,741 --> 00:35:11,420
back and forth through the shiv.

495
00:35:11,421 --> 00:35:16,460
If you wiped your finger on it, you would feel a little oily, greasy substance, and

496
00:35:16,461 --> 00:35:22,640
that is from the hemp-soaked rope that's in the middle of the steel cable.

497
00:35:22,641 --> 00:35:29,120
So no matter how sophisticated or simple the system, elevators will always need grease,

498
00:35:29,121 --> 00:35:36,100
which means Marshall will be around as long as people want to go up and down.

499
00:35:36,101 --> 00:35:40,640
Speaking of us, how about a high-altitude grease job, 10,000 feet high?

500
00:35:41,320 --> 00:35:43,360
We've got a lot of cable out there.

501
00:35:43,760 --> 00:35:47,060
And an underground look at America's most famous trolley cars.

502
00:35:51,220 --> 00:35:53,820
Grease helps us get from point A to point B,

503
00:35:54,300 --> 00:35:58,000
across oceans, across continents, or just across town.

504
00:35:58,980 --> 00:36:02,140
Public transportation systems have always relied on grease

505
00:36:02,141 --> 00:36:04,240
to move large numbers of people every day.

506
00:36:04,241 --> 00:36:14,080
railway grease for subways, axle grease for city buses, and cable grease for streetcars.

507
00:36:14,081 --> 00:36:20,400
In San Francisco, a network of underground cables pulls the cars up and down steel tracks.

508
00:36:20,401 --> 00:36:24,160
These are the last manually operated cable cars in the world.

509
00:36:24,161 --> 00:36:30,920
They were also the first, making their debut in the city by the bay in 1873, quickly popularizing

510
00:36:30,921 --> 00:36:33,160
this rope-hauled public transportation system.

511
00:36:34,380 --> 00:36:36,980
For the first hundred years, they used pine resin

512
00:36:36,981 --> 00:36:39,440
to lubricate the heavy cables that hauled the cars.

513
00:36:41,220 --> 00:36:44,000
Today, a synthetic rope grease coats the cables

514
00:36:44,001 --> 00:36:45,800
that run from an underground shiv room

515
00:36:45,801 --> 00:36:48,820
along the length of each of the three cable car routes.

516
00:36:50,320 --> 00:36:53,800
The longest one requires 22,000 feet of cable.

517
00:36:56,140 --> 00:36:59,000
A cable car trolley is the same basic concept

518
00:36:59,001 --> 00:37:00,320
as an aerial tramway.

519
00:37:00,920 --> 00:37:07,540
except here the track is a set of wire rope suspended above the ground in this

520
00:37:07,541 --> 00:37:14,920
case one that's 10,000 feet high the Sandia Peak tramway in Albuquerque New

521
00:37:14,921 --> 00:37:20,600
Mexico is one of the longest aerial trams in the world covering a distance of

522
00:37:20,601 --> 00:37:27,920
2.7 miles it takes passengers up to the breathtaking summit of the Sandia Peak

523
00:37:27,921 --> 00:37:31,440
Ladies and gentlemen, welcome to Sandia Peak Aerial Tramway.

524
00:37:33,680 --> 00:37:37,400
The tram makes its journey up and down the mountain four times an hour,

525
00:37:38,280 --> 00:37:40,960
carrying up to 10,000 pounds each trip.

526
00:37:42,080 --> 00:37:46,200
That's some serious friction, which means some serious grease.

527
00:37:46,201 --> 00:37:51,900
We lubricate almost every component we have with some form of a grease,

528
00:37:51,901 --> 00:37:56,620
from our electrical components to the tram cables

529
00:37:56,621 --> 00:38:00,580
to the bearings that turn these big wheels

530
00:38:00,581 --> 00:38:02,160
to the bearings in the motors.

531
00:38:03,120 --> 00:38:04,920
Like in most aerial systems,

532
00:38:05,500 --> 00:38:07,660
each of the two Sandia Peak tram cars

533
00:38:07,661 --> 00:38:10,060
sits on two fixed track cables

534
00:38:10,061 --> 00:38:12,960
that run on each side from the base to the summit.

535
00:38:13,820 --> 00:38:16,080
A third cable, called a haul cable,

536
00:38:16,720 --> 00:38:18,100
loops continuously around

537
00:38:18,101 --> 00:38:22,100
and pulls the tram cars up and down along the track cables.

538
00:38:22,101 --> 00:38:28,100
We've got a lot of cable out there, 15,000 feet each on all of the cables.

539
00:38:28,101 --> 00:38:32,100
Each of which requires some careful lubricating.

540
00:38:32,101 --> 00:38:38,100
Not an easy job when sections of these wire ropes hang almost two miles above solid ground.

541
00:38:38,101 --> 00:38:44,100
So the Sandia Peak maintenance team uses a unique cable greasing system,

542
00:38:44,101 --> 00:38:51,100
a high-pressure spray unit that attaches to the track cable behind the tram car.

543
00:38:51,101 --> 00:39:01,100
It has a wheel to guide it along a cable and two adjustable nozzles to adjust the spray

544
00:39:01,101 --> 00:39:05,100
so that the cable is covered more than two-thirds of its surface.

545
00:39:05,101 --> 00:39:12,100
As the tram car moves, the spray unit follows, dousing the cable with a specialized wire rope lube.

546
00:39:12,101 --> 00:39:19,100
With a 10,000 foot drop beneath them, these high wire grease monkeys are attached by safety harnesses at all times.

547
00:39:24,100 --> 00:39:29,100
For the whole cable that loops around, they use a similar high pressure oiler.

548
00:39:29,101 --> 00:39:34,100
But this one is fixed and lubricates the wire rope as it runs through it.

549
00:39:34,101 --> 00:39:40,100
It's got six nozzles in it and all six spray as the cable's going through it

550
00:39:40,101 --> 00:39:44,140
and totally covers the cable.

551
00:39:44,880 --> 00:39:47,040
While they grease the cables only once a year,

552
00:39:47,620 --> 00:39:49,560
the wheels that pull such heavy wire rope

553
00:39:49,561 --> 00:39:50,820
need weekly lubrication.

554
00:39:52,220 --> 00:39:55,040
The Sandia Peak tramways get another example

555
00:39:55,041 --> 00:39:57,260
of our modern-day lives' need for grease.

556
00:39:58,880 --> 00:40:01,860
But in this tribute to the modern marvel that is grease,

557
00:40:02,180 --> 00:40:05,020
we can't forget an unfortunate consequence

558
00:40:05,021 --> 00:40:06,820
of our dependence on it.

559
00:40:06,821 --> 00:40:12,820
Petroleum oil spills big and small have damaged coastlines all over the planet

560
00:40:12,821 --> 00:40:14,820
and the wildlife that inhabits them.

561
00:40:14,821 --> 00:40:18,820
Oil causes feathers to stick together and separate,

562
00:40:18,821 --> 00:40:21,820
so a bird loses heat and its ability to float.

563
00:40:21,821 --> 00:40:24,820
When a bird's covered with oil, it's really greasy,

564
00:40:24,821 --> 00:40:26,820
and it actually gets down to the skin.

565
00:40:26,821 --> 00:40:28,820
I mean, it's literally on their skin.

566
00:40:28,821 --> 00:40:30,820
So when it gets to the point of actually washing them,

567
00:40:30,821 --> 00:40:32,820
we have to get down with our fingertips.

568
00:40:32,821 --> 00:40:37,820
The International Bird Rescue Research Center in Northern California

569
00:40:37,821 --> 00:40:42,820
has been saving coastal birds after oil spills since the early 1970s.

570
00:40:42,821 --> 00:40:47,820
And for decades, their weapon of choice has been a common household grease cutter.

571
00:40:47,821 --> 00:40:52,820
Dawn performed better than anything, and it rinsed out of the feathers too.

572
00:40:52,821 --> 00:40:57,820
And it completely changed how we washed the birds and how many more survived.

573
00:40:57,821 --> 00:41:00,480
It turns out that Liquid Dawn's well-advertised

574
00:41:00,481 --> 00:41:02,420
grease-fighting power on dirty dishes

575
00:41:02,421 --> 00:41:06,280
works just as effectively on oil-covered animals

576
00:41:06,281 --> 00:41:08,220
without damaging their skins.

577
00:41:10,560 --> 00:41:13,700
Procter & Gamble now supplies IBRRC

578
00:41:13,701 --> 00:41:17,480
with Liquid Dawn for all its grease-fighting missions.

579
00:41:18,040 --> 00:41:20,820
So it kind of gets a big worry off our shoulders.

580
00:41:20,821 --> 00:41:22,520
We got the thing that's going to clean the birds.

581
00:41:23,960 --> 00:41:25,800
And with vegetable-based greases

582
00:41:25,801 --> 00:41:28,660
and other more environmentally friendly lubes on the rise,

583
00:41:29,400 --> 00:41:32,520
there may be less oil-covered birds in our future.

584
00:41:33,260 --> 00:41:36,080
That's a good thing, because in one form or another,

585
00:41:36,740 --> 00:41:41,680
Greece is sticking around to keep our world moving fast and friction-free.

586
00:41:55,800 --> 00:41:57,800
Oh!

587
00:41:57,801 --> 00:41:59,800
Oh-ha!

588
00:41:59,801 --> 00:42:01,800
Woo!

589
00:42:01,801 --> 00:42:03,800
Yeah!

